Procedure:

Choose decision making types (I chose HVAC, Appliance, and Shower types)
Choose criterion for MCDA and choose weightings for each criterion. Choose LLM; I chose Mistral Small 3.2 24B through OpenRouter as the LLM for all three architectures, with a temperature setting of 0.3 to decrease variability.
Create a set of scenarios
Choose amounts for each decision type by priority
Choose variables needed for calculations, in order to get an accurate picture of the energy consumption, environmental impact, etc. For my three decision types, I needed 
HVAC: Question, location, square footage, insulation, household size, utility budget, housing type, outdoor temperature, house age, R-value, HVAC age, SEER, occupancy context, alternative 1, alternative 2, and alternative 3.
Appliance: Description, location, utility budget, appliance, housing type, occupants, peak rate, off-peak rate, kWh per cycle, appliance age/type, baseline time, alternative 1, alternative 2, and alternative 3.
Shower: Description, location, occupants, tank size, gallons per minute (GPM), utility budget, housing type, outdoor temperature, water heater temperature, alternative 1, alternative 2, and alternative 3.
Create scenarios by first choosing the variables for each of the scenarios, then constructing an overall question based off of the variables chosen
Create a set of test scenarios by scaling down/generalizing information provided so that the AI has to make reasonable assumptions
For my three decision types, I removed from GT list (other things were generalized to ranges/words:
HVAC: R-value, HVAC age, SEER, and occupancy context
Appliance:  appliance, peak rate, off-peak rate, kilowatt-hours per cycle, appliance age, baseline time
Shower: tank size and water heater temperature
Make sure to leave scenarios for a future RAG database (I chose to keep 50 extra, which are not used in the test set. This leads to a total of 150 scenarios)
Create Ground-truth calculators for each decision type
Make functions to calculate the scores of each of your criterion
Make sure to first research reference ranges; what are the 5th and 95th percentile of values for the criterion you are calculating (e.g. carbon emissions & electricity usage for a dishwasher)
Then, research formulas; for behavioral criterion, understand how people react to certain times/usages/etc. For criterion with actual values, such as energy cost, use your predefined variables to calculate kWh. 
Create a MAVT function in each calculator (same for each). For value function type for each criterion, research how each criterion affects/is perceived by humans. For quantitative criteria, linear functions are appropriate. For behavioral criteria, a  logarithmic function is better. My final selections were linear for energy cost and environmental impact, and logarithmic (α=1.5 & 1.2 respectively) for comfort and practicality.
MAVT normalization will be done to each criteria based on your references ranges to a 0–10 scale by decision type
Aggregate all 4 criterion values based on weights into a single weighted sum, and then have each of the 3 alternatives in each scenario be ranked by value of the weighted sum
Run ground truth calculators on all 150 scenarios
Implement three architectures. Use identical MAVT weights for all: 
Pure Prompting: For each scenario, make 3 separate API calls (one per alternative). Each call provides the LLM only with the scenario text and asks it to output scores for all four criterion. No additional context, formulas, or reference data is provided. Output scores are weighted and summed for ranking
RAG-Enhanced: Same 3-call-per-scenario structure as Pure Prompting, but before scoring each alternative, retrieve the 3 most relevant chunks from a ChromaDB vector database containing the set aside 50 scenarios (mine were split into 25 HVAC, 20 Appliance, 5 Shower), which contain ground truth scores. Append retrieved context to the prompt before asking the LLM to score criteria. Output scores are weighted and summed for ranking
Create a program to build the RAG database by processing your 50 scenarios with ChromaDB for retrieval using the sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2 alternative. 
Hybrid: Make 1 API call per scenario (not per alternative). The LLM is asked only to extract parameters from the scenario text (such as setpoint temperature, flow rate, appliance type, usage time, etc.). Those parameters are passed directly to the LLM’s selected ground truth calculator from step 4, which computes all criterion scores based on the given parameters. MAVT weighting is then applied identically to ground truth calculation method.
For each architecture, compare output rankings against ground truth using Kendall's tau, Spearman's rho, Top-1 accuracy, Top-2 accuracy, MAE, and RMSE per criterion. Record total tokens, latency, and cost per decision for efficiency comparison.


