*Values are rounded for graphs and analysis. Exact values for all metrics can be found in metrics.
The Hybrid Architecture outscored both other architectures. The Pure Prompting architecture had almost random overall ranking and extreme error on every criterion across decision types. The RAG Enhanced architecture performed much better, but still not produce reliable levels of overall ranking





The Hybrid architecture had a Kendall's τ of 0.80 and Spearman's ρ of 0.82. This shows that the hybrid architecture had both a correct overall ranking order, and when the ranking was incorrect, the incorrect placement of the alternative was not far off of the true best position. RAG Enhanced was moderate at τ = 0.43, ρ = 0.49. Pure Prompting scored τ = −0.06, ρ = −0.055, which means that it was statistically indistinguishable from random and it provided no reliable ranking information.

Hybrid correctly identified the best alternative 83% of the time  and identified the best alternative in its top 2 picks 97% of the time. RAG-Enhanced had 58% and 89% respectively. Pure Prompting reached only 30% Top-1 accuracy, which is below the average (33%) from picking randomly. While its Top-2 was better at 62%, the overall usefulness of the architecture is low.







Hybrid produced the lowest overall MAE at 0.69, and RMSE at 1.52 (all on a 0 to 10 scale). While this metric isn’t as important for the end user, it does help identify how far off the architecture was on average (all three had better than random error) and how many large errors it made. RAG-Enhanced was intermediate at MAE = 1.61, RMSE = 2.40. Pure Prompting had the highest error at MAE = 2.52, RMSE = 3.07. The gap between Hybrid and Pure Prompting represents roughly a 1.8-point average error reduction per scenario. While Pure Prompting had the largest overall error amount, it had the smallest gap between RMSE (large errors penalized more) and MAE.















 



The above 6 graphs show some very interesting patterns on the overall scoring biases of each architecture. 
In HVAC scenarios (Top-1 = 92%, Top-2 = 100%, RMSE = 1.3, MAE = 0.62), where the deterministic calculator fully covered all pertinent parameters and additional estimation required very little LLM creativity, Hybrid performed best. In fact, on the comfort and practicality criterion, it had an overall MAE of 0.0! In the Appliance (Top-1 = 72%, Top-2 = 92%, RMSE = 2.1, MAE = 0.97) and Shower (Top-1 = 67%, Top-2 = 93%, RMSE = 1.07, MAE = 0.46) scenarios, where subjective factors like comfort and practicality necessitated partial AI estimation, it performed worse. RAG-Enhanced showed the same pattern among all of its Top-1, Top-2, RMSE, and MAE values as hybrid; its strongest ranking performance was also on HVAC even though Shower had lower RMSE and MAE, likely because HVAC scenarios have well-defined energy tradeoffs that map cleanly onto retrieved context.
 Pure Prompting, on the other hand, performed the worst on HVAC of all decision types (MAE = 2.11, RMSE = 2.55, Top-1 = 25%, below the 33% random baseline;). Even though the errors were smaller in magnitude than shower and appliance scenarios, every criterion value showed a bias in the wrong direction. This pushed the overall ranking of the alternative in a different direction and thus had a much larger effect on Top-1, Top-2, Kendall's τ, and Spearman's ρ.
Appliance and Shower decision types are clearly the most important for exposing different failure points for each architecture. Pure Prompting scored highest on Appliance–its only decision type with a positive tau–suggesting LLM general knowledge about appliance tradeoffs is slightly more reliable than for HVAC or Shower. However, its Appliance MAE (3.31) and RMSE (3.90) were the highest of any architecture-decision type combination. Despite having a lower MAE (1.34 vs. 1.46), RAG-Enhanced's Shower performance was noticeably worse than its HVAC performance. This suggests that the small Shower RAG database (5 scenarios) produced good average scores but failed to correctly rank alternatives within scenarios. Hybrid's Shower MAE (0.46) was the lowest of all decision types, but it showed the exact same ranking problem. 
However, in all of these analyses, there are partial biases with the decision type itself and the parameters selected; for instance, HVAC scenarios are much simpler to extract parameters for than appliance scenarios, so the patterns between decision types are not solely attributable to the architecture's quality.







	While Hybrid only needed 100 API calls (one per scenario), Pure Prompting and RAG-Enhanced needed 300 calls across 100 scenarios (three per scenario, one per alternative). Because it processed all three options in a single prompt, Hybrid used the most output tokens per call despite making fewer calls (16.3K total output vs. 10.0K for Pure). Due to the retrieval context appended to each call, RAG-Enhanced's input token count (295.2K) was almost 2.6× that of Pure (114.7K).




Hybrid's overall latency (275.6s across 100 scenarios) was lower than both Pure Prompting (437.1s) and RAG-Enhanced (480.8s) because each Hybrid call processed all three options in a single promptrather than sequentially. However, due to the larger single prompt, Hybrid had the longest average latency (2,756 ms/call) when compared to Pure(1,457 ms/call) and RAG (1,603 ms/call). Out of the three, RAG-Enhanced had the highest overall latency. The retrieval step added an average of about 146 ms per call over Pure, which added up to a total overhead of 43.7 seconds over all 300 calls.
The latency and token results collectively point to a clear cost-accuracy tradeoff that favors Hybrid for any household decision-support tool meant for real-world deployment. While Pure and RAG's 300-call structure would require parallelization to achieve the same responsiveness at scale, Hybrid is fast enough for real-time interaction—a user describing their situation and receiving a ranked recommendation in less than 3 seconds is practically feasible. Because of its 2.6× token overhead over Pure without a corresponding accuracy gain (τ = 0.43 vs.  negative 0.06), RAG-Enhanced is the least efficient of the three; the retrieval cost is only justified if the database is large and well-matched enough to consistently return relevant context, which the shower results suggest is not guaranteed with small domain-specific databases. Practically speaking, the Hybrid's 0% failure rate in all 100 scenarios is also significant because a tool that occasionally fails to generate a recommendation is worse than one that generates a slightly suboptimal one (and Hybrid fortunately does not suffer from this problem), and Pure and RAG's multi-call structure increases the surface area for partial failures even though none happened here.





While architectures receive generalized or categorical equivalents of the same fields, such as insulation quality in place of R-value and appliance age range in place of exact age, ground truth calculators receive fully specified numerical inputs. For instance, HVAC scenarios include exact R-value, HVAC age, and SEER rating, while appliance scenarios include precise kWh/cycle, peak and off-peak rates, and appliance age. The same pattern can be seen in shower scenarios, where architectures receive only a generalized GPM range while ground truth receives exact GPM and tank size. While all three of the architectures have to do the same level of interpretation, the Hybrid architecture is able to focus only on extraction and thus performs better. Additionally, Hybrid also doesn't have to deal with the main problem with most LLMs: specific numbers.
One conclusion is consistently supported by the data across all metrics: LLM scoring alone cannot take the place of physics-based calculation. With HVAC scenarios showed the best case scenarios, and Appliance and Shower representing its floor where partial AI estimation introduced residual ranking errors, Hybrid performed the best on all primary metrics (τ = 0.80, Top-1 = 83%, Top-2 = 97%, MAE = 0.69). RAG-Enhanced showed that retrieval context significantly outperforms unassisted scoring (τ = 0.43 vs. −0.06), but database size and quality continue to be a bottleneck, especially on Shower scenarios where its five-scenario database generated accurate average scores but failed within-scenario ranking. The most significant failure is revealed by Pure Prompting's nearly random overall performance (τ = −0.06) and simultaneous lowest MAE on HVAC with worst Top-1 on HVAC: systematic directional bias in criterion scoring that compounds across alternatives and collapses ranking accuracy even when individual errors appear numerically modest. With fewer total tokens, lower total latency, and a directly deployable single-call structure, Hybrid is the only architecture among the three that is practical for real-world household decision support, according to the token and latency results.










Conclusion
The hypothesis was largely confirmed; Hybrid achieved the strongest performance across all accuracy metrics (τ=0.80, ρ=0.82, Top-1=83%, MAE=0.69, RMSE=1.52), while Pure Prompting produced near-random rankings (τ=-0.06, Top-1=25-40% depending on decision type) and RAG-Enhanced fell in between (τ=0.43, Top-1=58%). That physics-based MCDA computation cannot be consistently replicated by end-to-end LLM scoring. Retrieval context is a useful tool, especially for HVAC scenarios where well-defined energy tradeoffs mapped cleanly onto the database (Top-1=63%, τ=0.52), as demonstrated by RAG-Enhanced's improvement over Pure Prompting. Retrieval alone, however, was insufficient for criteria that required subjective judgment: Pure Prompting's Shower environmental MAE of 4.04 was the only criterion-level error in the dataset that was higher than RAG's Appliance comfort MAE of 3.88. The difference between Hybrid (MAE=0.97) and Pure Prompting (MAE=3.31) was the biggest of any decision type in appliance scenarios, where exact quantitative reasoning about TOU rates and kWh/cycle is required.

One prediction was false: Hybrid used only 98,016 total tokens compared to Pure's 124,709, defying the theory that Hybrid would be the second most token-intensive due to structured output requirements, even though Hybrid used more output tokens per call. Because retrieval context was added to each prompt, RAG used the most tokens overall (308,637), and the 100-call structure compared to 300 calls for Pure and RAG more than made up for it. The fact that Hybrid was simultaneously the fastest, most accurate, and most token-efficient architecture (275.6s total latency vs. 437.1s for Pure and 480.8s for RAG) makes it the most practical architecture for real-world household energy decision support. The only major barrier to using a Hybrid type architecture is the immense research and time needed to actually build the calculators used by the architecture; in cases where perfect decision choices are not always needed, a lower cost RAG model (Top-2 = 89%) could be useful.







Next Step
The main engineering focus currently is to improve Hybrid's performance for appliance and shower scenarios, which are significantly behind HVAC performance (τ=0.68 and τ=0.60 compared to τ=0.90 for HVAC). The performance gap might be partly explained by the ability of occupancy context to enable completely deterministic extraction of comfort and practicality criteria, which resulted in a perfect 0.0 MAE for those criteria, while appliance and shower scenarios lack an analogous structural anchor. A concrete step forward would be to create scenario-type-specific extraction prompts rather than a general call for all types of scenarios. This would allow the appliance extractor to focus on scheduling context and the shower extractor to focus on duration and flow rates rather than trying to attend to all types simultaneously.
The other major generalizability constraint related to the parameters used is those specifically related to the state of Pennsylvania. The ground truth calculator uses a single data point for emissions factor (0.6458 lbs CO₂/kWh from EPA eGRID data) and PPL Electric's TOU pricing structure. As a result, architecture rankings might not generalize to other grid regions. A high renewables mix would compress environmental score differences between alternatives, which might affect which architecture performs best on this criterion. A larger set of parameters that includes all EPA eGRID subregions and real-time utility rates from the EIA API would allow us to test whether Hybrid's dominance is generalizable across energy contexts or is an artifact of the Pennsylvania energy mix.
The study was further limited to just one model. The Mistral Small 3.2 model was chosen for cost efficiency, but it is not clear if the advantages of Hybrid carry forward to more powerful models, nor is it clear if Pure Prompting can close the gap with a frontier model. If, in fact, a model with stronger reasoning capabilities can be expected to extract parameters and scoring criteria with high accuracy in one prompt, creating a physics based calculator for any decision type may be a waste of resources.
However, a more powerful model would be more costly. The same consideration can be applied to the converse situation, where if the parameters can be just as easily extracted by a lesser model, the advantages of Mistral Small 3.2, and its metrics, have not been optimized. The same 100 scenarios can be run against several model families to determine if the results represent architecture or model capabilities.
In the long term, the value of this tool lies in whether real households end up using this tool and whether it actually affects their decisions. The natural follow-up study on this topic would involve implementing a mobile interface for a group of households in Pennsylvania and tracking their decisions compared to the recommendations of this tool over a period of time. 




















