Well that's not really how MCDAs work. Just because one alternative is the best or worst in a certain criterion category does not mean it should automatically receive the best or worst score possible. This is because the other criterion categories matter too, which is why we rank each alternative across multiple criterion; these criterion have their own weightings, and based on how much each criterion matters (weighting) and the value assigned for each alternative for each criterion, we calculate the best alternative across all criterion.

